claimed: channel-drain — the half of the per-element cost never attacked. Profiling put 71% of Source.merge in the per-element channel transaction, and chunking cut it by dividing the COUNT on the send side, at the price of delaying an element (which is why it is opt-in). The receive side has no such price: taking what is ALREADY buffered delays nothing and changes no semantics, yet the consumer still pays one transaction per element. Add a drain that takes up to n buffered elements in ONE transaction, use it in the Channel Stream instance so Source.merge's output side batches invisibly, and measure. Expected ceiling: about half the 71%.
